  "summary": "Optical phenomenon where surfaces appear to change color as the viewing angle changes.",
  "description": "Created by thin-film interference or structural coloration, it produces rainbow-like effects seen in nature and human-made objects. Historically studied in optics and utilized in art, design, and fashion for its dynamic visual properties.",
  "culturalContext": "Iridescence — angle-dependent color from microstructure (soap bubbles, beetle shells, opals, abalone nacre) — became a material obsession: Art Nouveau Tiffany Favrile glass, Zsolnay eosin glazes, mother-of-pearl inlay traditions, beetle-wing embroidery (Mughal and Victorian), and holographic/pearlescent coatings from hot-rod flake to iPhone finishes. The physics (thin-film interference, photonic crystals) now drives bio-material research; visually, iridescence signals the boundary between nature and technology.",
